From fa59aea76a90a4315852da6d39590e0a68b73cfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Roscoe <bryanroscoe@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:50:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] AI: cache a projection that bails, unless the clock
 decided it

AiSession::get_or_project cached completed projections only. A key whose
projection bailed was projected again on every call, and in measurement mode
(no wall-clock budget) each attempt is a full traversal. EvasionRemovalPriority
asks for one at each target-selection node the planner's rollouts reach, so a
failing key is paid for at every one of them.

Seen in an all-AI four-player lab game (deck lab, Medium, measurement mode):
at turn 44, two players left and the AI at 1 life, a Bag of Holding rummage
discard sat on one key whose projection ran ~1.1 s and bailed with
GameOverDuringProjection, again and again; the AI made two moves in 600 s of
CPU. With bails cached that decision takes 4.3 s and the game finishes (a win
on turn 45, 77 s in all).

project_to is deterministic in its key once the clock is out of the picture,
so a bail other than TimeCapExceeded is stored in a separate negative cache
(projection_bail_cache) and returned as the same Err; TimeCapExceeded is still
never cached. Decision-neutral: a cached bail is the Err the recomputation
would return, with the same quick_state_hash collision tolerance the success
cache already has.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
